Quadcopters are a type of unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV)
that use four rotors to generate thrust and control their
motion. By adjusting the rotational speed of each rotor,
a quadcopter can take off vertically, hover, and maneuver
in different directions. Unlike fixed-wing aircraft,
quadcopters do not require forward motion to generate
lift. However, their flight dynamics can be complicated
because the forces and moments generated by the rotors
affect both the translational and rotational motion of
the aircraft.

For this project, our team aims to develop a
six-degree-of-freedom (6-DOF) digital twin of a
quadcopter to simulate its flight behavior under
different operating conditions. The model includes
three translational degrees of freedom along the
X, Y, and Z axes, as well as three rotational degrees
of freedom: roll, pitch, and yaw. Using Newton's
second law and angular momentum equations, we can
study how the forces and moments generated by the
four rotors influence the motion of the drone.

Our model is designed to complete three main flight
tasks. The first goal is to maintain a stable hover
at an altitude of 1 m for 120 seconds. The second
goal is to perform circular flight with a radius of
2 m at an altitude of 1 m and a speed of 0.5 m/s
for at least 60 seconds. The third goal is to complete
a flight sequence involving vertical takeoff, two
5 m straight flight segments, a 90-degree left yaw
rotation, hovering, and a controlled landing.
These tasks will allow us to evaluate the stability,
maneuverability, and overall performance of our model.

We selected the Holybro X500 V2 as the physical
reference for our digital twin. Its published
dimensions provide the geometric basis for the model,
while the mass moments of inertia and propulsion
coefficients are determined using component-based
calculations and available experimental data
\cite{holybro_x500,holybro_kits,uit_motor_thesis}.

\subsection*{Physical Parameters and Mass Distribution}

The Holybro X500 V2 has a diagonal wheelbase of
500 mm \cite{holybro_kits}. Therefore, the distance
from the center of the aircraft to each rotor is

\begin{equation}
l = \frac{0.500}{2} = 0.250\ \mathrm{m}.
\end{equation}

For a symmetric X-shaped configuration, the
horizontal coordinates of each rotor are

\begin{equation}
a = \frac{l}{\sqrt{2}}
  = 0.17678\ \mathrm{m}.
\end{equation}

The total mass of the quadcopter is modeled as
1.700 kg. Since the manufacturer does not specify
the mass distribution of the fully assembled aircraft,
we developed a component-based mass budget.
The assumed component masses and locations are
summarized in Table~\ref{tab:mass_budget}.

