design assumptions rather than manufacturer-verified
measurements. These assumptions allow the moments
of inertia to be calculated explicitly and updated
when more accurate component data become available.

\subsection*{Calculation of Mass Moments of Inertia}

The mass moments of inertia are calculated using
the parallel-axis theorem. For a component $j$
with mass $m_j$ and center-of-mass coordinates
$(x_j,y_j,z_j)$, its contribution to the aircraft's
inertia tensor is

\begin{align}
I_{xx} &=
\sum_j \left[
I_{xx,j}^{CM}
+m_j(y_j^2+z_j^2)
\right],\\
I_{yy} &=
\sum_j \left[
I_{yy,j}^{CM}
+m_j(x_j^2+z_j^2)
\right],\\
I_{zz} &=
\sum_j \left[
I_{zz,j}^{CM}
+m_j(x_j^2+y_j^2)
\right].
\end{align}

For the four motors, which are approximated as
point masses at $(\pm a,\pm a,0)$, the contributions
are

\begin{align}
I_{xx,\mathrm{motors}} &= 4m_{\mathrm{motor}}a^2,\\
I_{yy,\mathrm{motors}} &= 4m_{\mathrm{motor}}a^2,\\
I_{zz,\mathrm{motors}} &= 8m_{\mathrm{motor}}a^2.
\end{align}

The same method is used for the propellers and ESCs.
For the four radial arms, modeled as uniform rods,
the mean squared radial distance is

\begin{equation}
\overline{r^2} =
\frac{r_0^2+r_0r_1+r_1^2}{3}
=0.0285613\ \mathrm{m^2}.
\end{equation}

Their contributions are

\begin{align}
I_{xx,\mathrm{arms}} &=
I_{yy,\mathrm{arms}}
=\frac{m_{\mathrm{arms}}}{2}\overline{r^2},\\
I_{zz,\mathrm{arms}} &=
m_{\mathrm{arms}}\overline{r^2}.
\end{align}

For the rectangular central frame, battery,
and avionics, the local moments of inertia
are estimated using

\begin{align}
I_{xx}^{CM} &= \frac{m}{12}(w_y^2+h_z^2),\\
I_{yy}^{CM} &= \frac{m}{12}(w_x^2+h_z^2),\\
I_{zz}^{CM} &= \frac{m}{12}(w_x^2+w_y^2).
\end{align}

Summing the contributions gives the moments
of inertia about the initial reference plane:

\begin{align}
I_{xx,O} &= 0.017752\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2},\\
I_{yy,O} &= 0.018464\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2},\\
I_{zz,O} &= 0.034584\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2}.
\end{align}

The combined vertical center-of-mass location
is calculated from the component mass budget:

\begin{equation}
z_{CM} =
\frac{0.500(-0.035)+0.202(0.025)}{1.700}
=-0.007324\ \mathrm{m}.
\end{equation}

Applying the parallel-axis correction gives
the moments of inertia about the aircraft's
combined center of mass:

\begin{align}
I_{xx} &=
I_{xx,O}-mz_{CM}^2
=0.017661\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2},\\
I_{yy} &=
I_{yy,O}-mz_{CM}^2
=0.018373\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2},\\
I_{zz} &=
0.034584\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2}.
\end{align}

These values are calculated from the assumed
component mass distribution and are not direct
manufacturer measurements.

\subsection*{Rotor Spin Inertia}

The rotor spin inertia $J_r$ represents the
moment of inertia of the rotating assembly
about its spin axis.

For a two-blade propeller approximated as
two uniform slender blades, the propeller
contribution can be estimated as

\begin{equation}
J_{r,\mathrm{prop}} =
\frac{1}{3}m_{\mathrm{prop}}R^2.
\end{equation}

The 1045 propeller has a nominal diameter
of 10 inches, corresponding to a radius
of $R=0.127$ m. Assuming a propeller mass
of 0.013 kg gives

\begin{equation}
J_{r,\mathrm{prop}} =
\frac{1}{3}(0.013)(0.127)^2
=6.99\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2}.
\end{equation}

However, the complete rotating assembly also
includes the motor rotor. Therefore,

\begin{equation}
J_r = J_{r,\mathrm{prop}}
+J_{r,\mathrm{motor}}.
\end{equation}

The motor-rotor contribution has not yet been
determined from verified component data.
Consequently, the calculated propeller inertia
should not be treated as the validated inertia
of the complete rotating assembly.

\subsection*{Thrust and Aerodynamic Torque Coefficients}

The thrust and aerodynamic resistance torque
generated by each rotor are approximated by

\begin{align}
T_i &= k_T\omega_i^2,\\
Q_i &= k_Q\omega_i^2.
\end{align}

Published test data for the Holybro 2216 KV920
motor with a T1045 propeller provide thrust,
torque, and rotational speed measurements
\cite{uit_motor_thesis}. The data used for
the coefficient calculations are summarized
in Table~\ref{tab:motor_data}.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Published motor and propeller test data}
\label{tab:motor_data}
\begin{tabular}{ccc}
\toprule
\textbf{RPM} &
\textbf{Thrust (gf)} &
\textbf{Torque (N m)} \\
\midrule
4269 & 214.74 & 0.03 \\
6252 & 470.20 & 0.07 \\
10703 & 1367.04 & 0.19 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The rotational speed is converted from RPM
to radians per second using

\begin{equation}
\omega = \frac{2\pi}{60}\mathrm{RPM}.
\end{equation}

Thrust values reported in gram-force are
converted to newtons using

\begin{equation}
T(\mathrm{N}) =
T(\mathrm{gf})\frac{9.80665}{1000}.
\end{equation}

The coefficients are then determined using
a least-squares fit through the origin:

\begin{equation}
k =
\frac{\sum_i \omega_i^2 y_i}
{\sum_i \omega_i^4},
\end{equation}

where $y_i$ represents either the measured
thrust or torque.

The fitted coefficients are

\begin{align}
k_T &= 1.06775\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{kg\,m},\\
k_Q &= 1.52451\times10^{-7}\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2}.
\end{align}

The value of $k_Q$ assumes that the reported
torque can be used as an approximation of
the aerodynamic resistance torque. If the
reported measurement includes significant
motor losses, this coefficient would require
further correction.

